INT. CAPSIZED TRAWLER - AIR POCKET - NIGHT

A black, upside-down cabin, the floor now the ceiling. Water slaps a foot below. A single glow stick floats in a bucket, lighting the underside of everything green. A handheld radio is wedged in a bunk frame.

IMOGEN TAVARES, 40s, hangs from the rail by one arm, soaked, her other hand clamped around the radio. She breathes in ragged pulls.

IMOGEN
Pilar. Pilar, come in.

PILAR (O.S.)
(over radio)
I'm here.

IMOGEN
Where?

PILAR (O.S.)
(over radio)
Engine room. The hatch is jammed.

IMOGEN
How much air?

PILAR (O.S.)
(over radio)
Enough for a conversation.

IMOGEN
Don't joke.

PILAR (O.S.)
(over radio)
I'm not.

A beat. Water slaps. Imogen lowers herself down, wading chest-deep toward a bulkhead door, the radio held above her head.

IMOGEN
I'm coming.

PILAR (O.S.)
(over radio)
You can't swim that.

IMOGEN
Watch me.

She dives. The glow stick bobs. A thump. A muffled scream. The radio clicks.

PILAR (O.S.)
(over radio)
Imogen?

The bulkhead door bursts open and PILAR PERRAULT, 30s, surges through in a gush of water, gasping, hair plastered to her face. Imogen is behind her, one fist knotted in her collar. They slam against the wall, tangled, coughing, clutching each other, then shove apart.

PILAR
You left the hatch open.

IMOGEN
I left you alive.
